## 3. COMPLETELY REDESIGN THE CALENDAR

The calendar is the primary screen of the application. It must be simple, beautiful, readable, and immediately useful.

The current calendar design is unacceptable. Rebuild its visual presentation and interaction model rather than applying superficial CSS changes.

### Calendar requirements

- Display a clear monthly calendar as the primary interface.
- Provide intuitive previous-month and next-month navigation.
- Include an obvious way to return to the current month.
- Display the current month and year clearly.
- Use a conventional seven-column calendar with correctly aligned weekdays and dates.
- Correctly handle month boundaries, leap years, and dates from adjacent months.
- Make the current date visually distinguishable from selected dates and busy dates.
- Clearly distinguish available dates, manually blocked dates, and confirmed tour dates.
- Use a restrained, consistent color system with accessible contrast.
- Avoid overcrowding dates with text, excessive badges, icons, or unnecessary information.
- Make the calendar usable on desktop, tablet, and mobile.
- Keep the calendar as the visual centerpiece instead of surrounding it with unnecessary dashboard widgets.

### Calendar interactions

When I select a date, I should be able to:

- See whether the date is available or busy.
- See existing tours and their details.
- Mark the date as busy.
- Mark a manually blocked date as available again.
- Add a reason for the blocked date.
- Edit existing availability information.
- Add a tour manually.
- Edit an existing tour.
- Cancel or delete a tour with a clear confirmation step.
- Understand why a date cannot be made available if a confirmed tour still exists.

For a manually blocked date, I should be able to enter a reason such as:

"Private tour — Samarkand — unavailable for other bookings."

For a confirmed tour, I should be able to record:

- Tour date or date range.
- Client name.
- Destination.
- Tour description.
- Start time and end time, if applicable.
- Number of guests, if known.
- Booking source, if applicable.
- Additional notes.
- Reason for being unavailable, when useful.

Do not force me to fill in unnecessary fields when I only want to block a date.

### Date ranges

Support blocking multiple consecutive days.

For example, if I am unavailable on October 22 and October 23, I should be able to mark both dates as busy without creating two unrelated records manually.

Allow me to specify a start date and end date and apply the change to the entire period.

### Data integrity

A date can have multiple tours or overlapping reasons for being unavailable.

Never remove a busy date just because one tour was deleted or edited if another confirmed tour or manual block still requires that date to remain unavailable.

Separate the concepts of:

- Manually blocked availability.
- Confirmed tours.
- Booking inquiries that have not been accepted.

Do not treat an unconfirmed inquiry as a confirmed tour.

Do not silently overwrite existing booking details.

Do not allow an accidental click on a calendar date to cancel a confirmed reservation.

Use appropriate Firestore transactions or another reliable approach when multiple related records must be updated together.

The calendar must reflect saved data accurately after refresh, sign-in, and navigation between sections.

